  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Bonus free kicks after a goal",
  "description": "Seven against seven with goalkeepers on a pitch of about 60 × 45 metres. Put cones on three or four free kick spots in front of each goal: at an angle from the left, straight on, at an angle from the right, and ideally one inside the penalty area (indirect free kick).\n\nNormal match play, but every time a team scores from open play it gets two free kicks in a row as a bonus. The coach points out the spots. The opponents set up a wall of two or three players and the rest mark. A free kick goal is worth one point. Then play restarts from the goalkeeper who conceded.\n\nThe shooting team tries different solutions: a direct shot, a short pass to the side before the shot, a quick free kick before the wall is set, or a combination inside the penalty area. Variation: play with offside now and then – it changes where the receivers can stand. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Decide on the routine before you line up – and fool the wall with your body language."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "On a short free kick: pass firmly and flat, so the shooter can strike first time."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Look for the chance to take it quickly before the wall and the goalkeeper are set."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Follow up the shot – goalkeepers often spill free kicks."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
